%[text]{"align":"center"} # 
%[text]{"align":"center"} # Pendulum cart control
%[text] A mass $m${"editStyle":"visual"} is attached to a car of mass $M${"editStyle":"visual"} through a mass-less junction with a length $l${"editStyle":"visual"} . The car displacement is induced by a force u.  
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:1469)
%[text] The goal of the system is to stabilize the mass mp vertically, keeping the swing angle q2 = 0 by controlling the displacement q1 of the car. 
%[text] 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ### 1. Model of the system
%[text] The system has 2 degrees of freedom q1 and q2. 
%[text] Considering the (x,y) frame the position of the masses are given as 
%[text] $\\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{ll}\n\\vec{\\mathrm{OM}\\;}  & =q\_1 \\vec{\\;x} \\\\\n\\vec{\\;\\mathrm{Om}}  & =\\left(q\_1 -l\\;\\sin \\left(q\_2 \\right)\\right)\\;\\vec{x} +l\\;\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)\\;\\vec{\\;y} \n\\end{array}\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 
%[text] The velocities are:
%[text] $\\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{ll}\n\\frac{d\\vec{\\;\\mathrm{OM}} }{\\mathrm{dt}} & =\\dot{\\;q\_1 } \\;\\vec{\\;x} \\\\\n\\frac{d\\;\\vec{\\;\\mathrm{Om}} }{\\mathrm{dt}} & =\\left(\\dot{\\;q\_1 } +l\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\;\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)\\right)\\vec{\\;x} -l{\\dot{\\;q} }\_2 \\sin \\left(q\_2 \\right)\\;\\vec{\\;y} \n\\end{array}\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 
%[text] The kinetic energy of the system is given as 
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nT=\\frac{1}{2}M\\times v\_M^2 +\\frac{\\;1}{2}m\\times v\_{m\\;}^2 =\\frac{\\;1}{2}M{\\dot{\\;q\_1 } }^2 +\\frac{1}{2}{m\\left(\\left(\\dot{\\;q\_1 } +l\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\;\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)\\right)\\vec{\\;x} -l{\\dot{\\;q} }\_2 \\sin \\left(q\_2 \\right)\\;\\vec{\\;y} \\right)}^2 \\\\\nT=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(M+m\\right){\\dot{\\;q\_1 } }^2 +\\frac{1}{2}m\\left({l^2 \\dot{\\;q\_2 } }^2 -2l\\dot{\\;q\_1 } \\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)\\right)\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text] The potential energy of the system is produce only by the mass m as the mass M is already grounded. So we have
%[text] $V=\\mathrm{m}\\mathrm{g}\\mathrm{L}\\mathrm{c}\\mathrm{o}\\mathrm{s}\\left(q\_2 \\right)\\;${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 
%[text] From the about energies equation, we obtain the Lagrangian
%[text] $L=T-V=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(M+m\\right){\\dot{\\;q\_1 } }^2 +\\frac{1}{2}m\\left({l^2 \\dot{\\;q\_2 } }^2 -2l\\dot{\\;q\_1 } \\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)\\right)-\\mathrm{mgLcos}\\left(q\_2 \\right)\\;\\;${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] As the system is not only conservative. It also have dissipation force (friction of the contact between wheel and ground), generalized Lagrange equations apply as follows: 
%[text] $\\frac{d}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\;L}{\\partial \\;\\dot{\\;q\_i } }\\right)-\\frac{\\partial \\;L}{\\partial \\;q\_i }=Q\_i${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Rayleigh dissipation function will be used to introduce the friction force proportional to velocity. 
%[text] $R\\left(\\dot{\\;q} \\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sum\_i^N k\_i {\\;\\dot{\\;q\_i } }^2 =\\frac{1}{2}\\;\\left(k\_1 {\\dot{\\;q\_1 } }^2 +k\_2 \\;{\\dot{\\;q\_2 } }^2 \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] the dissipation force is given as $F\_i =-\\frac{\\partial R}{\\partial \\dot{\\;q\_i } }\\;\\;\\;;F\_1 =-\\frac{\\partial R}{\\partial \\dot{\\;q\_1 } }=-\\frac{1}{2}\*\\left({2\*k}\_1 \\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 } \\right)=-k\_1 \\;\\dot{q\_1 }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Thus $Q\_i \\;=F\_i +\<\\mathrm{other}\\;\\mathrm{external}\\;\\mathrm{forces}\\;\\mathrm{applied}\\;\\mathrm{to}\\;\\mathrm{particle}\\;i\>${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] - Lagrange equation w.r.t. $q\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}  \
%[text] 
%[text] $\\frac{d}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\;L}{\\partial \\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 } }\\right)-\\frac{\\partial \\;L}{\\partial \\;q\_1 }=-k\_1 \\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 } +u${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial \\;L}{\\partial \\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 } }=\\left(M+m\\right)\\dot{\\;q\_1 } -m\\;l\\;\\dot{q\_2 } \\;\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)\\;${"editStyle":"visual"};   
%[text] $\\frac{d}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\;L}{\\partial \\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 } }\\right)=\\left(M+m\\right)\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } -m\\;l\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } \\;\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)+m\\;l\\;{\\dot{q\_2 } }^2 \\;\\sin \\left(q\_2 \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $\\frac{\\partial \\;L}{\\partial \\;q\_1 }=0${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $\\Rightarrow \\frac{d}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\;L}{\\partial \\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 } }\\right)-\\frac{\\partial \\;L}{\\partial \\;q\_1 }=-k\\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 } \\;\\;\\Rightarrow \\left(M+m\\right)\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } -m\\;l\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } \\;\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)+m\\;l\\;{\\dot{q\_2 } }^2 \\;\\sin \\left(q\_2 \\right)+k\_1 \\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 } =u${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 
%[text] - Lagrange equation w.r.t. $q\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} \
%[text] $\\;\\Rightarrow -m\\;l\\;\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } \\;\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)+m\\;l^{2\\;} \\ddot{\\;q\_2 } -m\\;g\\;l\\;\\sin \\left(q\_2 \\right)=-k\_2 \\;\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\;${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 
%[text] 
%[text] The model of the system is given as 
%[text] $\\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{ll}\n\\;\\left(M+m\\right)\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } -m\\;l\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } \\;\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)+m\\;l\\;{\\dot{q\_2 } }^2 \\;\\sin \\left(q\_2 \\right)+k\_1 \\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 }  & =u\\\\\n-m\\;l\\;\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } \\;\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right)+m\\;l^{2\\;} \\ddot{\\;q\_2 } -m\\;g\\;l\\;\\sin \\left(q\_2 \\right)+k\_2 \\;\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\; & =0\n\\end{array}\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ### 2. Model linearization
%[text]  
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nq\_2 \\approx 0\\approx \\epsilon \\;\\\\\n\\dot{\\;q\_2 \\;} \\approx \\epsilon \\;\\;\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"} $\\Rightarrow {\\dot{q\_2 } }^2 =\\epsilon^2 \\;\\;;{\\dot{q\_2 } }^2 q\_2 =\\epsilon^3 =0${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $q\_2 \\approx 0\\;\\Rightarrow \\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{ll}\n\\cos \\left(q\_2 \\right) & \\approx 1\\\\\n\\sin \\left(q\_2 \\right) & \\approx q\_2 \n\\end{array}\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text] 
%[text] $\\Rightarrow \\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{ll}\n\\;\\left(M+m\\right)\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } -m\\;l\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } \\;+k\_1 \\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 }  & =u\\\\\n-m\\;l\\;\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } \\;+m\\;l^{2\\;} \\ddot{\\;q\_2 } -m\\;g\\;l\\;q\_2 +k\_2 \\;\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\; & =0\n\\end{array}\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text] 
%[text] $\\Rightarrow \\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{cc}\n\\ddot{\\;q\_1 }  & =\\frac{\\;m\\;l\\;}{M+m}\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } -\\frac{k\_1 }{M+m}\\;\\dot{\\;q\_1 } \\;+\\frac{1}{M+m}u\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\\\\n\\ddot{\\;q\_2 }  & \\;=\\frac{\\;1}{l}\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } -\\frac{\\;k\_2 }{m\\;l^2 }\\dot{\\;q\_2 } +\\frac{\\;g}{l}q\_2 \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\n\\end{array}\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text] 
%[text] $\\Rightarrow \\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{cc}\n\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } = & \\frac{\\;m\\;}{M+m}\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } \\;-\\frac{k\_2 }{\\left(M+m\\right)l}\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\;+\\frac{\\;m\\;g}{\\left(M+m\\right)}q\_2 \\;-\\frac{k\_1 }{M+m}\\dot{\\;q\_1 } +\\frac{1}{M+m}u\\\\\n\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } = & \\frac{\\;m}{M+m}\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } \\;-\\frac{\\;k\_1 }{\\left(M+m\\right)l}\\dot{\\;q\_1 } -\\frac{\\;k\_2 }{m\\;l^2 }\\dot{\\;q\_2 } +\\frac{\\;g}{l}q\_2 +\\frac{\\;1}{\\left(M+m\\right)l\\;}\\;u\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\n\\end{array}\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 
%[text] $\\Rightarrow \\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{cc}\nM\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } = & -k\_1 \\dot{\\;q\_1 } -\\frac{k\_2 }{l}\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\;+m\\;g\\;q\_2 \\;+u\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\\\\nM\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } = & \\;-\\frac{\\;k\_1 }{l}\\dot{\\;q\_1 } -\\frac{\\left(M+m\\right)\\;k\_2 }{m\\;l^2 }\\dot{\\;q\_2 } +\\frac{\\;\\left(M+m\\right)g}{l}q\_2 +\\frac{1}{l}u\\;\n\\end{array}\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 
%[text] $\\Rightarrow \\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{cc}\n\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } = & -\\frac{k\_1 }{M}\\dot{\\;q\_1 } -\\frac{k\_2 }{M\\;l\\;}\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\;+\\frac{\\;m\\;g}{M\\;}q\_2 \\;+\\frac{\\;1}{M}u\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\\\\n\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } = & \\;-\\frac{\\;k\_1 }{M\\;l}\\dot{\\;q\_1 } -\\frac{\\left(M+m\\right)\\;k\_2 }{M\\;m\\;l^2 }\\dot{\\;q\_2 } +\\frac{\\;\\left(M+m\\right)g}{M\\;l}q\_2 +\\frac{1}{M\\;l}u\\;\\;\n\\end{array}\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $\\;${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $\\Rightarrow \\left\\lbrace \\begin{array}{ll}\n\\dot{\\;q\_1 }  & =\\dot{\\;q\_1 } \\\\\n\\dot{\\;q\_2 }  & =\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\\\\n\\ddot{\\;q\_1 }  & =-\\frac{k\_1 }{M}\\dot{\\;q\_1 } -\\frac{k\_2 }{M\\;l}\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\;+\\frac{\\;m\\;g}{M\\;l}q\_2 \\;+\\frac{\\;1}{M\\;}u\\\\\n\\ddot{\\;q\_2 }  & =\\;-\\frac{\\;k\_1 }{M\\;l}\\dot{\\;q\_1 } -\\frac{\\left(M+m\\right)\\;k\_2 }{M\\;m\\;l^2 }\\dot{\\;q\_2 } +\\frac{\\;\\left(M+m\\right)g}{M\\;l}q\_2 +\\frac{1}{M\\;l}u\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\n\\end{array}\\;\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text] 
%[text] $\\left\\lbrack \\begin{array}{c}\n\\dot{\\;q\_1 } \\\\\n\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \\\\\n\\ddot{\\;q\_1 } \\\\\n\\ddot{\\;q\_2 } \n\\end{array}\\right\\rbrack =\\left\\lbrack \\begin{array}{cccc}\n0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\\\\n0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\\\\n0 & \\frac{\\;m\\;g}{M\\;} & -\\frac{\\;k\_1 }{M} & -\\frac{\\;k\_2 }{M\\;l}\\\\\n0 & \\frac{\\;\\left(M+m\\right)g}{M\\;l} & -\\frac{\\;k\_1 }{M\\;l} & -\\frac{\\left(M+m\\right)\\;k\_2 }{M\\;m\\;l^2 }\n\\end{array}\\right\\rbrack \\left\\lbrack \\begin{array}{c}\nq\_1 \\\\\nq\_2 \\\\\n\\dot{\\;q\_1 } \\\\\n\\dot{\\;q\_2 } \n\\end{array}\\right\\rbrack +\\left\\lbrack \\begin{array}{c}\n0\\\\\n0\\\\\n\\frac{\\;1}{M\\;}\\\\\n\\frac{1}{M\\;l}\n\\end{array}\\right\\rbrack u${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ### 3. Simulink Model of the system
%[text] The following values will taken for the system parameters
%[text] $M=1\\ldotp 5\\;\\mathrm{kg}\\;,m=0\\ldotp 5\\;\\mathrm{kg},\\;g=9\\ldotp 81\\;m/s\\;,\\;l=1\\;m,{\\;k}\_1 =k\_2 =0\\ldotp 01${"editStyle":"visual"}, 
%[text] We will take $q\_2 =y${"editStyle":"visual"} as the output of the system
%%

run("parameters.m");

[plant_ss, plant_tf, plant_zpk] = syst_repr_generator(params, 'q2');
plant_ss.StateName = {'q_1', 'q_2', '\dot{q_1}', '\dot{q_2}'}; 
q20 = 10;
Tsim = 10;
% plant_ss
plant_tf %[output:49f0445e]
dcgain(plant_tf) %[output:200d36ce]

plant_zpk %[output:5d4f9dd1]

%[text] ![](text:image:7d49)
%[text] ![](text:image:0def)
%%
%[text]{"align":"center"} ### 4. System analysis
%[text] #### 
%[text]  
%[text] #### Stability
poles_syst = pole(plant_ss) %[output:4e19ec27]
zeros_syst = zero(plant_ss) %[output:569f5570]
pzmap(plant_ss) %[output:5a8d975d]
%[text] The system is unstable because the second pole of the system is a real positive.
%[text] The only one zero of the system is negative making the system minimum phase.
%[text] #### 

rlocus(plant_ss) %[output:5a8d975d] %[output:3c977158]
%[text] $O\\;L\\;\\left(s\\right)=\\frac{N}{D}\\;\\;\\;;P\\;\\mathrm{ctrl}\\;;\\Rightarrow C\\;L\\;\\left(s\\right)=\\frac{P\\;N}{D+P\\;N\\;\\;}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] From the root locus we can conclude that a finite P controller only cannot stabilize the system. In fact, the system has 3 poles and 1 zero, so only one pole is pulled toward the zero as gain increases toward infinity. Even the fact that the two stable poles which diverge on imaginary axis, their centroid point is in left half plan, keeping them stable. However the unstable pole approaches the zero position in the left half plan only when P is infinitely high. So no finite P controller can stabilize the system. 
%[text] Thus a solution of control is to design a controller that take into account integral, derivative and proportional effects through loop shaping. 
%[text] #### 
%[text] #### Controllability and observability
run parameters.m
[plant_ss_q1] = syst_repr_generator(params,'q1');
[plant_ss_q2] = syst_repr_generator(params,'q2');
plant_ss = plant_ss_q1;
Qc = ctrb(plant_ss);

rc = rank(Qc)
ro_q1 = rank(obsv(plant_ss_q1))
ro_q2 = rank(obsv(plant_ss_q2))

%[text] The system is controllable as the rank of the controllability matrix is equal to the dimension of the A. 
%[text] $r\_o =3\\not= \\dim \\left(A\\right)=4${"editStyle":"visual"}, the system is not observable when $q\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} is chosen as the output of the system. However it become observable for $q\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}as output 
%[text] 
plant_ss_diag = diagonalize(plant_ss)
%[text] By diagonalizing the LTI model, we can directly see that all the state are controllable as B matrix is full. 
%[text] 
%%
%[text]{"align":"center"} ### 5.Control 
%[text] #### Loop shaping with PID controller
%[text] In this section simulink Control System Designer tool is used to tune a PID controller that stabilize the pendulum. 
%[text] ![](text:image:99ec)
%[text] For the simulation $q\_{20} =10\\degree${"editStyle":"visual"}
Tsim = 10;
q20 = 10; 
Ts = 1e-3;
q2_f = deg2rad(-10);
IC = [0,deg2rad(10),0,0];
model = 1; % 1 : linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model
[plant_ss] = syst_repr_generator(params,'q2');
input_select = plant_ss.C(1:2);
sim("models/mdl_with_PID.slx");
refs = {'q1_ref', 'q2_ref'};
n = 1; 
figure(n);
animate_pendulum_cart(x.Time, x.Data(:,1), x.Data(:,2), u.Data,  params, 'fps', 100, string(refs(find(input_select==1))), ref.Data*input_select') %[output:0baf074b] %[output:33b96067]
%%
%[text] #### State feedback control
%[text] This control law is expressed as $u=-Kx+k\_{\\mathrm{s}\\mathrm{c}\\mathrm{a}\\mathrm{l}\\mathrm{e}} \\;y\_{\\mathrm{r}\\mathrm{e}\\mathrm{f}}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[output:1985865c] %[output:226c13d8] %[output:5aa6c372] %[output:948ae464]
%[text] 
%[text] Classical state feedback controller is only applicable to SISO system, so we have to choose one of the two possible outputs
%[text] #### State feedback controller with cart position (q\_1) as output 
%[text] By default we will set $k\_{\\mathrm{s}\\mathrm{c}\\mathrm{a}\\mathrm{l}\\mathrm{e}} =1${"editStyle":"visual"}
[plant_ss]  = syst_repr_generator(params,'q1');
poles_des = [-3, -3, -3, -3];
rank(plant_ss.B);
K = acker(plant_ss.A,plant_ss.B, poles_des);
Tsim = 20;
Ts = 1e-3;
q2_f = deg2rad(10);
IC = [0,deg2rad(20),0,0];
model = 1; % 1 : linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model
k_scale = 1;
input_select = plant_ss.C(1:2);
refs = {'q1_ref', 'q2_ref'};
sim('models/mdl_with_State_Feedback.slx');
n = 1;
n = n+1;
clf(gcf) %[output:86c63f98]
figure(n);
animate_pendulum_cart(x.Time, x.Data(:,1), x.Data(:,2), u.Data,  params, 'fps', 100, string(refs(find(input_select==1))), ref.Data*input_select') %[output:503dcd60] %[output:86c63f98]
%[text] With the compensator $k\_{\\mathrm{scale}}${"editStyle":"visual"} set to default 1, a state feedback controller cannot assure a null static error.
%%
%[text] To illustrate the compensator effect, let's consider a simple first order system with a static gain of 0.5. The step plot show a response ceilling at 0.5 instead of 1, the reference. With the compensator, the output is scaled to catch the reference. The same principle is used in state feedback controller.
s = zpk('s');
toy_system = 2/(4 + s) %[output:8e504529]
clf(gcf) %[output:49ae1558]
figure %[output:1d58f5ab]
step(toy_system) %[output:1d58f5ab]
figure %[output:1b7c6dce]
clf(gcf) %[output:1b7c6dce]
dcg = dcgain(systs) %[output:6e83dcc6]
compensator = 1/dcg %[output:57fdcc64]
step(compensator*systs) %[output:1b7c6dce]

%[text] By default we will set $k\_{\\mathrm{s}\\mathrm{c}\\mathrm{a}\\mathrm{l}\\mathrm{e}} =\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{d}\\mathrm{c}\\mathrm{g}\\mathrm{a}\\mathrm{i}\\mathrm{n}\\left(\\mathrm{c}\\mathrm{l}\\mathrm{o}\\mathrm{s}\\mathrm{e}\\mathrm{d}\\;\\mathrm{l}\\mathrm{o}\\mathrm{o}\\mathrm{p}\\right)}${"editStyle":"visual"}
[plant_ss]  = syst_repr_generator(params,'q1');
poles_des = [-3, -3, -3, -3];
rank(plant_ss.B);
K = acker(plant_ss.A,plant_ss.B, poles_des);
Tsim = 20;
Ts = 1e-4;
q2_f = deg2rad(20);
IC = [0,deg2rad(20),0,0];
model = 1; % 1 : linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model

A_cl = plant_ss.A - plant_ss.B*K;
B_cl = plant_ss.B;
closed_loop_ss =  ss(A_cl, B_cl, plant_ss.C, 0);
[b,a] = ss2tf(A_cl,B_cl, plant_ss.C,0);
closed_loop_tf = tf(b,a) %[output:4a4edec4]
dcg = dcgain(closed_loop_ss);
k_scale = 1/dcg;
input_select = plant_ss.C(1:2);
refs = {'q1_ref', 'q2_ref'};

sim('models/mdl_with_State_Feedback.slx');
clf(gcf);
n = n+1; 
figure;
animate_pendulum_cart(x.Time, x.Data(:,1), x.Data(:,2), u.Data,  params, 'fps', 100, string(refs(find(input_select==1))), ref.Data*input_select') %[output:8c6de65b] %[output:49ae1558]

%%
Tsim = 10;
Ts = 1e-4;
q2_f = deg2rad(20);
IC = [0,deg2rad(20),0,0];
model = 1; % 1 : linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model
input_select = plant_ss.C(1:2);
sim('models/mdl_with_State_Feedback.slx');

refs = {'q1_ref', 'q2_ref'};
n = n+1; 
clf(gcf) %[output:33410c03]
figure(n);
animate_pendulum_cart(x.Time, x.Data(:,1), x.Data(:,2), u.Data,  params, 'fps', 100, string(refs(find(input_select==1))), ref.Data*input_select') %[output:86df9932] %[output:33410c03]
%%
%[text] #### State feedback controller with swing angle (q\_2) as output 
%[text] Taking $q\_2${"editStyle":"visual"}as output of the system reveals one of the implication of non observability show above. Actually, state feedback control only changes system's poles (the denominator of the the transfer function). Looking closely to the transfer function's numerator when $q\_2${"editStyle":"visual"}is the output, we notice that $b\_0 =-1\\ldotp 866e-16\\approx 0${"editStyle":"visual"}, which result in $\\mathrm{dcgain}=\\frac{b\_0 }{a\_0 }\\;${"editStyle":"visual"} being null and an infinite compensator $k\_{\\mathrm{scale}} =\\infty${"editStyle":"visual"}
[plant_ss]  = syst_repr_generator(params,'q2');
poles_des = [-3, -3, -3, -3];
rank(plant_ss.B);
K = acker(plant_ss.A,plant_ss.B, poles_des);
Tsim = 20;
Ts = 1e-4;
q2_f = deg2rad(20);
IC = [0,deg2rad(20),0,0];
model = 1; % 1 : linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model

A_cl = plant_ss.A - plant_ss.B*K;
B_cl = plant_ss.B;
closed_loop_ss =  ss(A_cl, B_cl, plant_ss.C, 0);
[b,a] = ss2tf(A_cl,B_cl, plant_ss.C,0);
closed_loop_tf = tf(b,a) %[output:685213d4]
dcg = dcgain(closed_loop_ss) %[output:03967ca9]
k_scale = 1/dcg %[output:7172e2ff]
%[text] 
%%
%[text] #### LQR state feedback control
[plant_ss]  = syst_repr_generator(params,'q1');
Q = 1000*eye(4);       
R  =0.001;
K = lqr(plant_ss.A, plant_ss.B, Q, R);
A_cl = plant_ss.A - plant_ss.B*K;
B_cl = plant_ss.B;
closed_loop =  ss(A_cl, B_cl, plant_ss.C, 0);

dcg = dcgain(closed_loop);
k_scale = 1/dcg;
Tsim = 20;
Ts = 1e-4;
q2_f = deg2rad(10);
IC = [0,deg2rad(10),0,0];
model = 3; % 1 : linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model

input_select = plant_ss.C(1:2);
sim('models/mdl_with_State_Feedback.slx');
refs = {'q1_ref', 'q2_ref'};
n = n+1; 
clf(gcf) %[output:4f49e5da]
figure(n);
animate_pendulum_cart(x.Time, x.Data(:,1), x.Data(:,2), u.Data,  params, 'fps', 100, string(refs(find(input_select==1))), ref.Data*input_select') %[output:4c1e0dd7] %[output:4f49e5da]

%%
%[text] #### 
%[text] #### Observer based state feedback control
%[text] In reality the state are not directly accessible. They are either measured using sensors or estimated with techniques such Luenberger for deterministic systems and Kalman filter for stochastic systems. 
%[text] In this exercise will first start with Luenberger observer.
%[text] This observer expressed the observed states as : 
%[text] $\\dot{x\_o } =A{\\;x}\_o +B\\;u\\;+L\\left(y-y\_o \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}  ; $y\_o =C\\;x\_o \\;\\;\\mathrm{a}\\mathrm{n}\\mathrm{d}\\;y=C\\;x${"editStyle":"visual"} $\\;\\Rightarrow \\;${"editStyle":"visual"}$\\dot{\\;x\_o } =\\left(A-L\\;C\\right)\\;x\_o +B\\;u+L\\;y\\;${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text] where $y\_o \\;\\mathrm{and}\\;y${"editStyle":"visual"} are respectively the observed and real output. L is the Luenberger gain that can be calculated via pole placement in order the guaranty a response time of the observer 5 to 10 times faster than the closed loop of the system. 

[plant_ss]  = syst_repr_generator(params,'q1');
poles_des = [-3, -3, -3, -3];
rank(plant_ss.B);
K = acker(plant_ss.A,plant_ss.B, poles_des);
Tsim = 20;
Ts = 1e-4;
q2_f = deg2rad(20);
IC = [0,deg2rad(20),0,0];
model = 1; % 1 : linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model

A_cl = plant_ss.A - plant_ss.B*K;
B_cl = plant_ss.B;
closed_loop =  ss(A_cl, B_cl, plant_ss.C, 0);
obsv_poles = 5*poles_des;
L = acker(plant_ss.A', plant_ss.C', obsv_poles);
dcg = dcgain(closed_loop);
k_scale = 1/dcg;
input_select = plant_ss.C(1:2);
refs = {'q1_ref', 'q2_ref'};
model = 1;
sim('models/mdl_with_State_Feedback_and_observer.slx');
clf(gcf); %[output:0c0c60ed]
n = n+1; 
figure(n);
animate_pendulum_cart(x.Time, x.Data(:,1), x.Data(:,2), u.Data,  params, 'fps', 100, string(refs(find(input_select==1))), ref.Data*input_select') %[output:8e24230d] %[output:0c0c60ed]

%%
%[text] #### LQR State feedback with integrale action
%[text] In order to assure that static error elimination on the pendulum angle, we are going to augment the controller with integral action. 
%[text] 
% Augmented system (integrale action on q2)
[plant_ss]  = syst_repr_generator(params,'q2');
P = [plant_ss.A, zeros(4,1); -plant_ss.C, 0]; % -C_q2
Rin = [plant_ss.B; 0];
Q = 2*eye(5);
R = 2.5;
K_aug = lqr(P, Rin, Q, R) %[output:63e9252a]
P_cl = P - Rin*K_aug %[output:9f502086]
R_cl = Rin  %[output:32d9220b]
ss_cl =  ss(P_cl, R_cl, [plant_ss.C 0], 0);
pole(ss_cl) %[output:2a1b52e1]

Tsim = 20;
Ts = 1e-4;
IC = [0,deg2rad(1),0,0];
model = 1; % 1: linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model
input_select = [0,1];
q2_f = deg2rad(10);
sim('models/mdl_with_State_Feedback_integrale.slx')
clf(gcf) %[output:4cd38a5c]
figure
animate_pendulum_cart(x.Time, x.Data(:,1), x.Data(:,2), u.Data,  params, 'fps', 100, string(refs(find(input_select==1))), ref.Data*input_select') %[output:0b648183] %[output:4cd38a5c]


%%
%[text] #### Sliding mode control
%[text] Sliding mode control is a robust control strategy that aims to drag the system dynamic to a sliding surface 
%[text] 
run parameters.m
[plant_ss]  = syst_repr_generator(params,'q1');
Qc = ctrb(plant_ss.A,plant_ss.B);
e = [zeros(1,3) 1];
p = 3;
lambda = (plant_ss.A + p*eye(4))^3;
F = e*inv(Qc)*lambda; 
k0 = 20;
phi = 0.5;
q1_ref = 5; 
IC = [0,deg2rad(10),0,0];
q2_f = deg2rad(2);
M = [plant_ss.A, plant_ss.B; F, 0];
x_u_inf = M\[zeros(4,1); 1];
alpha = plant_ss.C*x_u_inf(1:end-1);
r = q1_ref/alpha; 
input_select = plant_ss.C(1:2);
Tsim = 20;
Ts=1e-4;
model = 1; % 1 : linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model
sim('models/mdl_with_SMC.slx');
refs = {'q1_ref', 'q2_ref'};

clf(gcf) %[output:1836065a]
figure
animate_pendulum_cart(x.Time, x.Data(:,1), x.Data(:,2), u.Data,  params, 'fps', 30, string(refs(find(input_select==1))), ref.Data*input_select')%, 'VideoName', "output_smc_tanh_Linear_mdl.mp4") %[output:1a68da75] %[output:1836065a]

run parameters.m
[plant_ss]  = syst_repr_generator(params,'q1');
Qc = ctrb(plant_ss.A,plant_ss.B);
e = [zeros(1,3) 1];
p = 5;
lambda = (plant_ss.A + p*eye(4))^3;
F = e*inv(Qc)*lambda; 
k0 = 50;
phi = 0.5;
q1_ref = 5; 
IC = [0,deg2rad(10),0,0];
q2_f = deg2rad(2);
M = [plant_ss.A, plant_ss.B; F, 0];
x_u_inf = M\[zeros(4,1); 1];
alpha = plant_ss.C*x_u_inf(1:end-1);
r = q1_ref/alpha; 
input_select = plant_ss.C(1:2);
Tsim = 20;
Ts=1e-4;
model = 3; % 1 : linear model       2: linear model diagonalized        3: non linear model
sim('models/mdl_with_SMC.slx');
refs = {'q1_ref', 'q2_ref'};

clf(gcf) %[output:37b6ff4c]
figure
animate_pendulum_cart(x.Time, x.Data(:,1), x.Data(:,2), u.Data,  params, 'fps', 30, string(refs(find(input_select==1))), ref.Data*input_select') %, 'VideoName', "output_smc_tanh_NLinear_mdl.mp4") %[output:5dc3f670] %[output:37b6ff4c]

%%
%[text] #### RST control
Ts = 1e-4
discrete_syst, discrete_b = c2dm(plant_ss.A, plant_ss.B, plant_ss.C, plant_ss.D, Ts, "tustin")


